WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:09.750
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: May we turn to item eight,
Paul, the Consolidated Month Four

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:14.530
&gt;&gt;Paul Baumann: Thank you Malcolm. The report
you have in front of you shows the financial

00:00:14.530 --> 00:00:19.869
position as it was round about a month ago,
in the quirky nature of the deadlines of this

00:00:19.869 --> 00:00:27.070
Board meeting and the reporting process. Intriguingly
enough, I get the answers for the latest estimates

00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:32.870
and year to date tomorrow morning, which I
will obviously share with you as soon as I

00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:34.250
have the opportunity.

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:39.390
As it says in the paper, we have a major stock
take planned on the back of the numbers that

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:44.230
I get tomorrow, with the exact team meeting
on 1st October to have a thorough root- and

00:00:44.230 --> 00:00:48.490
branch look through the numbers as they are
emerging; the trends, and where necessary

00:00:48.490 --> 00:00:55.010
the mitigations that we need to take to bring
things back into line. This is in a year which,

00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:59.280
as Simon has already said, is looking very
challenging; certainly for the commissioning

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:04.710
sector, but even more challenging I think
for the provider sector. So having a real

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:09.509
handle and a real grip on all of this early
in the year, relatively speaking for the NHS,

00:01:09.509 --> 00:01:12.389
is quite an important thing.

00:01:12.389 --> 00:01:16.759
For all of those reasons I suggest the Board
treats the numbers that are presented in this

00:01:16.759 --> 00:01:21.969
report with a degree of caution, because they
are out of date, and as you know we have done

00:01:21.969 --> 00:01:28.189
quite a lot of work now to get the predictive
capability in finance slightly sharper by

00:01:28.189 --> 00:01:33.709
looking at some of the future predictive trends,
both in governance of finance and in some

00:01:33.709 --> 00:01:39.729
of the cost-spend trends. I am slightly concerned
that this may be showing in particular around

00:01:39.729 --> 00:01:44.619
CCGs and one or two of our direct commissioning
teams a more favourable picture than we will

00:01:44.619 --> 00:01:48.909
be looking at when I see the numbers tomorrow
morning, but we will see where that takes

00:01:48.909 --> 00:01:50.200
us.

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:53.829
As you know, our primary objective in the
commissioning sector is to live within the

00:01:53.829 --> 00:01:57.740
magic number, which is shown at the bottom
either of appendix A if you like detail, or

00:01:57.740 --> 00:02:06.109
in table one if you like less detail. So the
£101,167 million - to be overly precise - that

00:02:06.109 --> 00:02:10.750
we have available at the level of the non-ring
fenced revenue expenditure limit, which is

00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:16.450
what we are predominantly held to account
for, is the core performance metric.

00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:21.950
You will see in those two tables that, as
at month four, there were no significant year-to-date

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:28.150
variances, and there were actually few CCGs
or direct commissioning teams that were at

00:02:28.150 --> 00:02:33.989
month four predicting significant variances
from their plans for the year. In these numbers

00:02:33.989 --> 00:02:38.260
at least, there are just two significant issues
to draw to your attention. One of which is

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:42.700
a technical one, which is a mismatch in our
depreciation budget which I have explained

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:49.099
before, which does lead us to have underspend
on depreciation which we cannot count towards

00:02:49.099 --> 00:02:54.319
our main performance metric. That is a shame
because it is money we cannot deploy behind

00:02:54.319 --> 00:02:58.750
the things that we would want to spend it
on. It comes through as the negative at the

00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:00.409
bottom of the table.

00:03:00.409 --> 00:03:04.459
The second thing which I talked about last
time, and do not propose to go into any further

00:03:04.459 --> 00:03:09.569
today, is the Cancer Drugs Fund. £70 million
overspend predicted for this year, against

00:03:09.569 --> 00:03:15.030
an already increased budget for the year.
As you know, we have taken action in recent

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:21.969
weeks to correct by a re-prioritisation process
for the Cancer Drugs Fund, but because of

00:03:21.969 --> 00:03:26.569
the length of time that these things take
to impact and the protections that are in

00:03:26.569 --> 00:03:31.730
place for existing patients within the Cancer
Drugs Fund, the impact on the overspend for

00:03:31.730 --> 00:03:39.390
2015/16 is very small. Those are the two major
things that figure in the month four numbers,

00:03:39.390 --> 00:03:44.280
leading to that overspend of £85 million
that you can see at the bottom of the table.

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:50.280
As you know, at the same time we do the core
forecast we look carefully at the risks attached

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:54.319
to that position and the mitigations that
we have got available, either in the form

00:03:54.319 --> 00:03:59.639
of corrective action that we can deploy, or
reserves and contingencies that can be used.

00:03:59.639 --> 00:04:04.849
At the time of the month four forecast these
were indicating a net risk, as are set out

00:04:04.849 --> 00:04:11.249
in the report, of a further £183 million
on top of that £85 million. So as we come

00:04:11.249 --> 00:04:17.780
into month five the starting point is that
we have got at least £268 million of corrective

00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:23.610
action to take, even if there are no trends
within month five which take us in the wrong

00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:29.420
direction. So lots of work to do across the
commissioning sector to live within our core

00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:35.200
spending limit, even before we think about
the wider concerns around the financial position

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:38.600
in the NHS at large.

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:42.170
This is of course a significant challenge
for us for the very reason that our operating

00:04:42.170 --> 00:04:47.770
plans for this year were set exceptionally
tightly. There are not significant reserves

00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:53.720
sitting there waiting to be deployed, especially
in specialised commissioning where we had

00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:59.900
to keep very tight budgets in order to meet
the affordability criteria, and a number of

00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:04.700
CCGs where from the start of the year it has
been very clear that they are less resilient,

00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:11.440
less able to cope with shocks than their counterparts
elsewhere in the country. We will work very

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:16.140
hard on that. I do anticipate, when I come
back to the next Board meeting, being able

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:22.110
to report that we have substantially closed
the gap of £268 million that you get if you

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:28.020
combine the forecast with the attendant risks.
However, I say so with anticipation that we

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:33.220
are going to have to have done a lot of hard
work with CCGs and with partners in the provider

00:05:33.220 --> 00:05:37.640
sector in order to do that. That is probably
all I want to say at this stage, because it

00:05:37.640 --> 00:05:45.050
will be more interesting when I've got the
more up-to-date numbers in a day or two's

00:05:45.050 --> 00:05:45.960
time.

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:58.440
&gt;&gt;Moira Gibb: Paul, in paragraph 3.6 on QIPP.
Last year we achieved a delivery rate of 87%

00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:11.040
and this year we are forecasting 95%. What
have we done in the system to enable that

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:12.850
higher level of delivery?

00:06:12.850 --> 00:06:16.880
&gt;&gt;Paul Baumann: Well, I guess you are comparing
a current forecast to an actual from last

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:23.560
year. There is a slight hazard in my answer
to your question, but I do anticipate that

00:06:23.560 --> 00:06:27.360
we will be better this year in terms of delivery
of QIPP; predominantly because we have spent

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:31.650
a hell of a lot of time in the planning process,
as we have discussed at various committee

00:06:31.650 --> 00:06:37.270
meetings before subjecting the QIPP plans
to pressure testing, making sure that in the

00:06:37.270 --> 00:06:42.460
interaction between our local teams and the
CCGs there was the maximum possible sharing

00:06:42.460 --> 00:06:48.200
of best practice and so on. So I think we
have, broadly speaking, a stronger set of

00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:53.480
QIPP plans coming into this year than we have
had in previous years, but there is no denying

00:06:53.480 --> 00:06:58.830
that it is a difficult set of things to do.
Remember also, the QIPP plans are very closely

00:06:58.830 --> 00:07:04.170
correlated with planning and grip on activity.
Again, as we have discussed at previous meetings

00:07:04.170 --> 00:07:11.110
here, we were very much more interventionist,
if I can put it that way, in the activity

00:07:11.110 --> 00:07:15.320
plans that CCGs have set this year - and indeed
specialised commissioning has set for this

00:07:15.320 --> 00:07:19.150
year - because we have learned the lessons
from previous years, where that was overly

00:07:19.150 --> 00:07:24.470
ambitious if you like, in terms of the correction
factors that come in. I think there is reason

00:07:24.470 --> 00:07:28.760
to believe that we have a better grip on it
this year than in previous years. I hope I

00:07:28.760 --> 00:07:30.530
will still be saying that when we get to month
12.

00:07:30.530 --> 00:07:37.170
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: I am just going to underline
one fact for the Board that is implicit in

00:07:37.170 --> 00:07:40.930
what both Barbara and Paul have said, but
perhaps has not shone through quite as strongly,

00:07:40.930 --> 00:07:47.240
which is a small ray of sunshine, which is
that the emergency activity growth in the

00:07:47.240 --> 00:07:54.430
first four months of this year has continued
to be more modest than the average four last

00:07:54.430 --> 00:08:00.370
year. I think the figure is that we have seen
emergency admissions growth of 0.4% compared

00:08:00.370 --> 00:08:08.330
with the 2.3% that CCGs have funded capacity
and providers. Obviously we do not know how

00:08:08.330 --> 00:08:12.620
that will play out for the year as a whole,
but it does imply that the deceleration in

00:08:12.620 --> 00:08:17.420
growth that we saw in emergency activity towards
the end of the last financial year has been

00:08:17.420 --> 00:08:22.580
sustained into the first four months of this
year, and obviously that is good news from

00:08:22.580 --> 00:08:27.190
the point of view of both provider capacity
finances and CCGs.

00:08:31.380 --> 00:08:34.860
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Paul, thank you very much.
We note the report with all the caveats that

00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:39.890
you have identified for us, and we will follow
it closely obviously at future meetings. Thank

00:08:39.890 --> 00:08:41.030
you very much for that

